    <string name="chapter_portage_intro">1836. Between the lower lake and the upper one, the river drops over a ledge of rapids no boat can run. Everything that travels between the two waters is unloaded at Port Ellery, carted over the portage road and loaded again at Carrick Landing.\n\nA few merchants in both towns have put up the money for a railway across the portage, one of the first in the country. Lay a short line through the gorge beside the rapids and run the first train.\n\nCarry people between the two landings, and bring timber from the camp up the east shore across to the sawmill at Port Ellery. You have until the end of 1846.</string>
    <string name="chapter_trunk_intro">1856. Five towns sit along the north shore of the lake, from Ashcombe in the west to Saint-Évrard in the east, with Marlowe, the biggest, between them. In winter the lake freezes and the stage road is the only way through, slow and deep in snow or mud.\n\nThe province has granted a charter for a trunk line along the shore. Four rivers come down to the lake on the way, and each needs a bridge.\n\nJoin the chain: carry passengers from both ends to Marlowe, bring the mail in, and help Marlowe grow to 1,200 people by the end of 1868. A stage company already runs coaches along the shore road.</string>
    <string name="chapter_last_spike_intro">1881. Port Haldane on the west coast has been promised a railway to the rest of the country. Between it and Fort Calder on the prairie stand two ranges of mountains, with a long valley between them and only two passes low enough to climb.\n\nThe Western Slope Railway is building east from the coast, and the government wants the line joined within five years. Winter brings deep snow to the passes, and on the steep slopes high up it lets go in avalanches.\n\nCarry 1,000 passengers a year between Port Haldane and Fort Calder, and bring 3,000 tonnes of coal from the mine in the valley to the port, by the end of 1886.</string>
    <string name="chapter_seaway_intro">1954. A great lake in the west drains down a long river to the sea in the east, but halfway along it the river falls over a ledge of rock. Ships from the lake go no further, and ships from the sea turn back below the rapids.\n\nDig a canal across the ledge, with two locks up onto it at each end, and open the river to ships from end to end. Ice closes it every winter.\n\nBring 15,000 tonnes of iron ore from the mine north of the lake to the steel mill downstream, and 12,000 tonnes of grain from the farms by the lake to the port at the river mouth, by the end of 1964.</string>
    <string name="chapter_iron_ore_intro">1955. A huge body of iron ore lies under Carrow Lake, far inland on the Shield. Between it and the sea at Baie-Clairval is rough rock, muskeg and lakes in every direction, with no road and no rail.\n\nBuild a railway from the mine to the coast, over the rock and round the lakes. Winters are long and cold, and the snow comes early.\n\nBring 15,000 tonnes of ore to the steel mill at Baie-Clairval, and help the town grow to 1,000 people, by the end of 1965.</string>
    <string name="chapter_high_speed_intro">2032. Westbridge and Montrevel, the two biggest cities in the province, sit at either end of a long lake shore, with smaller towns along the way. Corridor Coachways runs its coaches on the highway between them.\n\nBuild a fast line along the corridor and take the travellers off the road. The fastest trains of the day run at 200 km/h, on straight, level track.\n\nCarry 8,000 passengers a year between Westbridge and Montrevel, and build the company up to be worth $2,000,000, by the end of 2042.</string>
    <string name="chapter_warming_north_intro">2062. The winters in the north are shorter every year. Tamarack Point and Fort Delorme, on lakes out in the tundra, are reached in winter by ice roads over the lakes, and those close earlier each spring, and the railhead at Fort Ashdown is far to the south.\n\nThe towns here only grow when they get what they need: people moving about, mail, food all year, and fuel for heat through the winter once they're big enough.\n\nKeep both northern towns supplied and help each grow to 1,500 people by the end of 2072.</string>
